# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): the activities and sources below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://github.com/rspeer/wordfreq> ,
              <https://en.wiktionary.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The deck was checked by machine (scripts checking the counts, the ids, that every back is Arabic letters and vowel marks only, NFC and the uniqueness of every front in every language; the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators and a second SHACL validator) and by AI agents; it has had no human review."@en ,
                 "Ranking. Every word of wordfreq 3.1.1's Arabic list (the 100 000 most frequent, from Wikipedia, film and TV subtitles, news, the web and Twitter) was matched, without its vowel marks and, where needed, without its clitics (و ف ب ل ك ال, the future سـ, the object and possessive suffixes), against every form of every Arabic noun, verb, adjective and function word in English Wiktionary, inflection tables included. Its frequency was shared among the entries it matched, weighted towards dictionary and principal forms and against entries whose senses are all archaic or rare, and the entries were ranked by the frequency they collected."@en ,
                 "Selection. An AI agent chose the 300 adjectives a learner of Modern Standard Arabic needs, the corpus rank its main evidence, dropping the method's artifacts (a rare word credited with a particle's frequency, such as مَنّ 'aphid' with the preposition من), archaic, Classical-only, rare, dialect-only and vulgar words, proper names, and elatives filed as entries of their own (أَكْبَر, أَعْلَى: they are forms of other adjectives), all but 12 nationality adjectives, and narrow news and technical terms, to make room for everyday words (colors, sizes, feelings, the weather, qualities). The order is the ranking's, moved by hand only where a homograph inflated or buried a word."@en ,
                 "Writing the cards. The back is English Wiktionary's headword, the masculine singular, fully voweled as Wiktionary gives it, at a pause (no case ending on nouns and adjectives). The note gives the headword's transliteration, Wiktionary's own converted to ALA-LC (ā ī ū, th kh dh sh gh, ʻ for ayn, ʼ for hamza, no initial hamza, ta marbuta -ah, a final -iyy as -ī), and forms chosen from Wiktionary's entry: the feminine and the usual plural (the broken plural first where there is one), each in Arabic and transliterated; the note's labels are in the reader's language, the forms the same in all. The front gives one to three meanings in each of six languages, the most common sense first, written by AI agents from the sense chosen and Wiktionary's glosses, verbs as infinitives (To write, Att skriva, schreiben, Escribir, Écrire, 쓰다). Bracketed qualifiers tell apart words that would otherwise share a front, near-synonyms mostly. Every front is unique in each language in the deck."@en ,
                 "Card ids are the ALA-LC transliteration in ASCII letters, without diacritics, ʻ, ʼ or hyphens (كِتَاب kitab, كَتَبَ kataba, كَبِير kabir, مَدِينَة madinah); a clash gets -2, then -3, in rank order. They are addresses, not shown as text."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the deck is CC BY-SA 4.0. Its ranking uses wordfreq's data, CC BY-SA 4.0. Its Arabic, forms and transliterations, and the English glosses its meanings were written from, come from English Wiktionary, CC BY-SA 4.0, through Wiktextract's extract on kaikki.org. The share-alike sources set the deck's licence; both are credited here."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Selection (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The 300 adjectives, chosen by an AI agent from the ranking and checked against every candidate's Wiktionary entry; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: About 45 artifacts of the ranking dropped (آلِيّ credited with إلى, أَلَذّ with الذي, غَيُور with غير and the like), with the elatives filed as their own entries and narrow news and technical terms; everyday words brought in from deep in the ranking (hungry, tired, dirty, the colors); 12 nationality adjectives kept (Arab, American, Egyptian, Saudi, French, English, German, Chinese, Russian, European, Spanish, Japanese)."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Fronts and forms in six languages (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Every card's six fronts and the forms its note shows: written by one AI agent per 100 cards, then checked by a second that back-translated every front into Arabic against Wiktionary's glosses and checked every form and the Arabic; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 18 corrections over the 300 cards, 16 of them to fronts, the others to the forms shown; 27 doubts about the Arabic passed on to the next round."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Arabic forms (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Every doubt the writers and reviewers raised about a card's Arabic (its voweling, transliteration or the forms its note shows): decided by an AI agent against English Wiktionary, and every change checked letter by letter and mark by mark by a second agent; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Doubts about 27 cards: 26 changed (mostly a feminine or plural missing from Wiktionary's headword line, added from its entry), 1 kept, each change confirmed by the verifier. A final full-deck review of every back and note by another AI agent found 19 more errors: the voweling of إِنْجِلِيزِيّ, a case ending on the plural بُسَطَاء, and the ALA-LC spelling of a medial ِيّ (īy, not iyy) in 17 plurals, made uniform by script."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Distinct fronts and machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The whole deck: fronts shared by two cards in any language, found by script and told apart by an AI agent; then the scripts' checks, the library checker (npm run library) with the app's shapes, DCAT-AP and SKOS, and a second SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck)."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 16 fronts were shared by two cards in some language; 9 cards got a new front in one or more languages. A final review of the whole deck, one AI agent per language, then changed 28 fronts (Korean 22, most to the -다 dictionary form of -적이다 adjectives, German 5, French 1). The release conforms: the scripts found every front unique in each language, every back and form fully voweled Arabic in NFC and every id unique; the library checker found the library valid with this deck in it, and pySHACL found all 228 documents it checks conform."@en .

<https://github.com/rspeer/wordfreq>
    dcterms:title "wordfreq 3.1.1 (Arabic word frequencies from Wikipedia, film and TV subtitles, news, the web and Twitter)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Robyn Speer" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Content: the frequencies of the 100 000 most frequent Arabic words (wordlist 'best'), which rank the deck. Its README states: “wordfreq is freely redistributable under the Apache license (see LICENSE.txt), and it includes data files that may be redistributed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 license.”"@en .

<https://en.wiktionary.org/>
    dcterms:title "English Wiktionary (Arabic entries, as extracted by Wiktextract on kaikki.org)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wiktionary contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Content: the Arabic headwords with their vowel marks, their forms (plurals, feminines, present tenses, verbal nouns, verb forms) and transliterations, and the English glosses the meanings were written from; downloaded from kaikki.org on 2026-10-14. Text is available under CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wiktionary:Copyrights)."@en .

